 some of this depends on it.\n\n**Issues**\n\n1. **`SELECT *`**: It returns every column from both tables, including `id` and other names that appear in both. Most clients then have to deal with the duplicates. It also pulls data you probably don't need, and it stops a covering index from being used. List the columns you need.\n2. **`lower(u.email) = ...`**: Wrapping the column in a function stops a normal index on `email` from being used, so this scans the whole table. Either:\n   - create a functional index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`\n   - store emails normalized to lowercase, or use a case-insensitive type (`citext` in Postgres), and compare directly.\n3. **No aliases on the output and no ordering**: If you want a stable result, add an `ORDER BY`, for example `o.created_at DESC`.\n4. **Hardcoded literal**: If this runs from application code, use a bind parameter (`$1` or `:email`). That avoids SQL injection and lets the database reuse the query plan.\n5. **Index on `orders.user_id`**: Make sure it exists, or the join will be slow for users with many orders.\n\n**Suggested rewrite** (Postgres-style, with example columns):\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id AS order_id,\n       o.created_at,\n       o.total,\n       u.id AS user_id,\n       u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower($1)\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC;\n```\n\nThis needs the supporting indexes:\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nIf you tell me which database you use and which columns you need, I can adjust the query. I can also edit `query.sql` directly if you'd like.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}